Natural Language Processing on Azure:

How machines understand text
Computers work with numbers, not words. So text has to be converted first. The classic approach: tokenization — split text into tokens (words or subwords), then assign each a number. Example: "I love C#" → [42, 891, 334].

But a plain number misses meaning. The breakthrough: word embeddings — map each token to a point in high-dimensional space where meaning determines proximity. "King" and "Queen" are close. "King − Man + Woman ≈ Queen." That's the geometry of language models.
Modern LLMs use transformers (the "T" in GPT, BERT). The key innovation: attention — when processing a word, the model looks at every other word in the sentence and weighs how relevant each is. In "The bank by the river was steep," attention figures out "bank" = riverbank, not financial institution, from context. This is what makes transformer-based NLP so much better than older approaches.
